# Day 26 — Wazuh SSH Brute-Force Detection & Alert Investigation 🛡️

## 🎯 Objective

Today I practiced how a SOC analyst investigates repeated SSH authentication failures using Wazuh and Linux logs.

Topics covered:

- SSH authentication monitoring
- Identifying repeated failed logins
- Source IP and username investigation
- Wazuh alert analysis
- Event timeline creation
- Correlating failed and successful logins
- Writing a SOC investigation report

> **Safety:** Perform all tests only on your own Linux VM or an authorized lab.

---

## 1. What Is SSH Brute Force?

SSH brute force is an attack technique in which someone repeatedly attempts to authenticate to an SSH service using different passwords or usernames.

Example:

```text
SSH Service
    ↓
Failed Login
    ↓
Failed Login
    ↓
Failed Login
    ↓
Repeated Authentication Attempts
    ↓
Investigate for Possible Brute Force
```

Repeated failures may indicate brute-force activity, but they can also result from a forgotten password, an incorrectly configured service, or a monitoring system. Always investigate the context.

