## 3. Check SSH Authentication Logs

On your Ubuntu lab machine, run:

```bash
sudo grep -Ei "Failed password|Invalid user" /var/log/auth.log | tail -n 30
```

This searches for common SSH authentication failure messages.

To count failed-password entries:

```bash
sudo grep -c "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
```

To view successful SSH authentication entries:

```bash
sudo grep "Accepted" /var/log/auth.log | tail -n 20
```

**Note:** These commands assume your system stores authentication logs in `/var/log/auth.log`. On other distributions, use `journalctl` or the appropriate authentication log.

For SSH service events:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ssh --since "1 hour ago"
```

If your service is named `sshd`, use:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u sshd --since "1 hour ago"
```

